: 'persons', 'value': 'Leary, Timothy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ram Dass (1931- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-04-04T22:17:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Obits', 'section_name': 'Obituaries', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ead6fc8-137d-5334-be12-b350ac1c4739', 'word_count': 1258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ead6fc8-137d-5334-be12-b350ac1c4739'}, {'abstract': 'It’s almost impossible to ingest opioids by accident, but misinformation has triggered a panic about the risks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/04/opinion/fentanyl-opioids-exposure.html', 'snippet': 'It’s almost impossible to ingest opioids by accident, but misinformation has triggered a panic about the risks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As baseless public health scares go, the one about police officers and nurses purportedly overdosing from passive fentanyl exposure should have been easy to dispel. Emergency workers across the country have reported dozens of such incidents in recent years, but their symptoms are often inconsistent with opioid poisoning. In some cases, officers have administered the overdose-reversal drug Narcan to themselves — a feat that would be impossible in the midst of an actual overdose. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/04/opinion/04fentanyl/fileupload-1554305564533-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/04/04/opinion/04fentan