'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Staffan', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Qvist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pinker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/08494003-36d1-5d78-8ec9-243ed117d959', 'word_count': 1480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/08494003-36d1-5d78-8ec9-243ed117d959'}, {'abstract': 'Fighting the dragon queen Tiamat is a much more satisfying way to spend time with my friends than social media ever was.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/06/opinion/sunday/dungeons-and-dragons.html', 'snippet': 'Fighting the dragon queen Tiamat is a much more satisfying way to spend time with my friends than social media ever was.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I started playing Dungeons & Dragons right around the time I completely gave up on Facebook. It was a little less than a year ago, as the first stories broke about the Cambridge Analytica scandal. I was sick of the social media idea of friendship, defined as likes or shares or “X knows the same 50 people you know.” So when my friend Kate suggested we start a game of Dungeons & Dragons, I thought, “Yes, I’m going to get together with people face-to-face, without any hearting or retweeting, and we’re going to eat chips and fight those damn cultists who are trying to resurrect the evil, five-headed dragon queen Tiamat.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/07/opinion/sunday/07newitz/65f5ce4875e94a64b164d072d9fda1c8-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 510, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/04/07/opinion/sunday/07newitz/65f5ce4875e94a64b164d072d9fda1c8-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 510}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/07/opinion/sunday/07newitz/65f5ce4875e94a64b164d072d9fda1c8-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 871, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/07/opinion/sunday/07newitz/65f5ce4875e94a64b164d072d9fda1c8-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1741, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/07/opinion/sunday/07newitz/07newitz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/04/07/opinion/sunday/07newitz/07newitz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/07/opinion/sunday/07newitz/07newitz-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why the Cool Kids Are Playing Dungeons & Dragons', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Cool Kids Play Dungeons &amp; Dragons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dungeons & Dragons (Game)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wizards of the Coast Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-04-06T18:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk'