ue': 'Iran', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Islamic Revolutionary Guards Corps', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khamenei, Ali', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khomeini, Ruhollah', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-04-08T10:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian H. Hook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'Hook', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5bcd30f-b045-520b-b81a-9fc7443acb4d', 'word_count': 985, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5bcd30f-b045-520b-b81a-9fc7443acb4d'}, {'abstract': 'The toxic disinformation of social media has rendered traditional forms of humor quaint and futile.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/08/opinion/the-end-of-satire.html', 'snippet': 'The toxic disinformation of social media has rendered traditional forms of humor quaint and futile.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — In January 2015, not long after I arrived here to live and teach philosophy, terrorists assassinated 12 people, including four cartoonists, in an attack on the offices of the French satirical newspaper Charlie Hebdo. This act brought to the surface and seemingly galvanized a view about censorship I had long held. I spent considerable energy in the weeks and months after defending the absolute right of satirists to keep doing what I saw as their sacred work, and criticizing many of my former friends, who found it more important at that moment to speak out against offending the sensitivities of religious communities, for their moral cowardice in the face of nihilistic violence.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/08/opinion/08stoneWeb/merlin_139127196_833311da-5990-45d7-a76d-16c7a7fe8c37-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/04/08/opinion/08stoneWeb/merlin_139127196_833311da-5990-45d7-a76d-16c7a7fe8c37-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/08/opinion/08stoneWeb/merlin_139127196_833311da-5990-45d7-a76d-16c7a7fe8c37-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/08/opinion/08stoneWeb/merlin_139127196_833311da-5990-45d7-a76d-16c7a7fe8c37-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/08/opinion/08stoneWeb/08stoneWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/04/08/opinion/08stoneWeb/08stoneWeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/08/opinion/08stoneWeb/08stoneWeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The End of Satire', 'kicker': 'THE STONE', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-04-08T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin E.H. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'H', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5947f793-2f58-501d-83f1-bf61fdde222c', 'word_count': 1668, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5947f793-2f58-501d-83f1-bf61fdde222c'}, {'abstract': 'The U.S. president needs to keep cooperating closely with Mexico, steer clear of any military action in Venezuela and refrain from bullying his partners and allies in the region.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/08/opinion/trump-latin-america-immigration.html', 'snippet': 'The U.S. president needs to keep cooperating closely with Mexico, steer clear of any military action in Venezuela and refrain from bullying his partners and allies in the region.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 2020 United States presidential campaign is already in full swing in Latin America. Four hot-button issues that helped propel President Trump to the White House in 2016 are already coming into play in his approach with the region, and they are being driven by two parallel electoral strategies: appeals to his political base nationwide on immigration, drugs and trade, and a hard sell directed at South Florida on Cuba and Venezuela.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/08/opinion/08shifterWeb/merlin_153115107_91e761b9-5cf5-4e1a-80dc-138aca9d1840-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019