icle', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sharon Otterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sharon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Otterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ddf5998e-22e0-5f94-aab7-50835a095171', 'word_count': 971, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ddf5998e-22e0-5f94-aab7-50835a095171'}, {'abstract': '“The House of the Pain of Others,” by the Mexican writer Julián Herbert, revisits the murder of 300 Chinese immigrants over three days in 1911, during the Mexican Revolution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/09/books/review-house-of-pain-of-others-julian-herbert.html', 'snippet': '“The House of the Pain of Others,” by the Mexican writer Julián Herbert, revisits the murder of 300 Chinese immigrants over three days in 1911, during the Mexican Revolution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Delve into the books of the Mexican writer and musician Julián Herbert and how obedient — how chaste — so much contemporary nonfiction can suddenly seem in comparison. Does Herbert write memoirs? Essays? Novels? His books are mash-ups of memory, investigation and fictional ornamentation, marked with a fond disrespect for genre — much like life.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/10/books/09bookherbert2/09bookherbert2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 806, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/04/10/books/09bookherbert2/09bookherbert2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 806}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/10/books/09bookherbert2/09bookherbert2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 762, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name