, 'value': 'India', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-04-13T15:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gyan Prakash', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gyan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Prakash', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5280a23-6dff-5a48-8798-d29c22f9f969', 'word_count': 1103, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5280a23-6dff-5a48-8798-d29c22f9f969'}, {'abstract': 'Life under the unbreakable lure of the screen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/13/opinion/sunday/the-infinite-scroll.html', 'snippet': 'Life under the unbreakable lure of the screen.', 'lead_paragraph': ' “Phoning it in” means to do something with very little effort or enthusiasm. The idiom seems oddly prescient now, given that it dates back to a time before the internet. (If you’re curious, further details are a mere Google away.) Is humanity now phoning in life? These photographs, taken in New York City late last month, show clearly what most people know: We are much more likely to be looking at our digital slabs than at our fellow human beings.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/10/opinion/sunday/10exposures-privacy7/merlin_153200736_326e5881-6efe-4f03-8947-c9ccf66fe4a5-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/04/10/opinion/sunday/10exposures-privacy7/merlin_153200736_326e5881-6efe-4f03-8947-c9ccf66fe4a5-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2