tion_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Turkewitz and Jack Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Turkewitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d8a3783-54f8-5f32-b777-75feb778f290', 'word_count': 1442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d8a3783-54f8-5f32-b777-75feb778f290'}, {'abstract': 'She was a healthy 35-year-old when she fell ill with a series of symptoms, including crimson blistering on her face and body.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/17/magazine/diagnosis-doctors-believed-she-had-leukemia-but-could-that-explain-her-horrible-rash.html', 'snippet': 'She was a healthy 35-year-old when she fell ill with a series of symptoms, including crimson blistering on her face and body.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The woman watched the streetlights flash by as the ambulance carried her through the midnight roads of her hometown, Bay City, Mich., to Karmanos Cancer Institute in Detroit. Her husband was in the front seat talking with the driver. He wasn’t allowed to ride in the back with her. But he had been at her side for the past four weeks, ever since she found out she had ulcerative colitis.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/21/magazine/21mag-Diagnosis-1/21mag-Diagnosis-1-articleLarge.png', 'height': 643, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/04/21/magazine/21mag-Diagnosis-1/21mag-Diagnosis-1-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 643}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', '