09:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Helen Ellis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Helen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ellis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd5e2878-2514-5aad-9f2f-ff621ac11721', 'word_count': 956, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd5e2878-2514-5aad-9f2f-ff621ac11721'}, {'abstract': 'Marilyn Stasio’s Crime column features a new and gruesome Lucas Davenport novel and the latest appearance of Parnell Hall’s cantankerous murder-solving puzzle lady.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/19/books/review/new-crime-fiction.html', 'snippet': 'Marilyn Stasio’s Crime column features a new and gruesome Lucas Davenport novel and the latest appearance of Parnell Hall’s cantankerous murder-solving puzzle lady.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m down with cannibals. Snakes, not so much. So it wasn’t until the end of John Sandford’s new Lucas Davenport novel, NEON PREY (Putnam, $29), when that cannibal looks into the “cold black eyes of a Crotalus scutulatus,” that I felt well and truly revolted. Sandford has never been one to shrink from violence; and until that point, he’d been his usual entertaining self in this hard-core adventure. He shines, in fact, when writing about partially eaten corpses rotting in the Louisiana marshes, bullet-riddled bodies baking in the Las Vegas desert, and one especially bloody shootout with the main bad guy blasting into “a virtual wall of humanity” in a crowded Vegas shopping mall.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/21/books/review/21Crime/21Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarg