'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Sifton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sifton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b6d6657-bd04-5c58-950a-a11ed6c5b92d', 'word_count': 666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b6d6657-bd04-5c58-950a-a11ed6c5b92d'}, {'abstract': 'Melissa Rivero’s debut novel, “The Affairs of the Falcóns,” features a family that, like hers did, emigrates to New York from Peru.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/21/books/affairs-of-falcons-melissa-rivero-interview.html', 'snippet': 'Melissa Rivero’s debut novel, “The Affairs of the Falcóns,” features a family that, like hers did, emigrates to New York from Peru.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Melissa Rivero is a lawyer and now a debut novelist. As a child, she was an undocumented immigrant from Peru, living in New York City. Her book, “The Affairs of the Falcóns,” is about Ana and Lucho, a married couple who, in the 1990s, flee a tumultuous Peru with their two children to live in New York. The novel focuses on Ana and her attempts to forge a future while navigating the hurdles faced by an undocumented resident. Despite the parallels to her own family’s experience, Rivero didn’t set out to write an autobiographical story, even though it was initially inspired by an anecdote from her mother. Below, Rivero talks about how becoming a parent changed the book, what she wants her own children to learn from the novel and more.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/22/books/22rivero1/21rivero1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 874, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/04/22/books/22rivero1/21rivero1-articleLarge