issions', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Liberalism (US Politics)', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Bill', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Caps and Emissions Trading Programs', 'rank': 19, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-04-25T09:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/1adae45c-87fc-5501-97fe-7a103621d5f6', 'word_count': 841, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1adae45c-87fc-5501-97fe-7a103621d5f6'}, {'abstract': 'An ordinary morning in the life of a parent dropping his son off at school assumes mythic proportions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/25/magazine/poem-the-scream.html', 'snippet': 'An ordinary morning in the life of a parent dropping his son off at school assumes mythic proportions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By its very nature, the hybrid form of the prose poem alerts us to the blurring of boundaries. In Fady Joudah’s “The Scream,” an ordinary morning in the life of a parent dropping his son off at school assumes mythic proportions: A typical altercation between two kindergartners escalates into a conflict as elemental and disturbing as the parables in Genesis. For one charged moment, the well-worn rules governing domination and fear are flipped: Who is to be pitied and who judged? At what point does blame turn feral and we retreat, self-satisfied, behind our collective censure? Selected by Rita Dove', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/28/magazine/28mag-poem-1/28mag-poem-1-jumbo.png', 'height': 572, 'width': 581, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': No