e, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'American Messiahs: False Prophets of a Damned Nation (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-04-26T09:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Molly Worthen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Molly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Worthen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d1fd196-6997-5497-8ef1-f84b2ac262ed', 'word_count': 1338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d1fd196-6997-5497-8ef1-f84b2ac262ed'}, {'abstract': 'The artist Frédéric Pajak’s hybrid book “Uncertain Manifesto” is haunted by the philosopher’s encounters with fascism, among much else, as these illustrated panels show.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/26/books/review/frederic-pajak-uncertain-manifesto.html', 'snippet': 'The artist Frédéric Pajak’s hybrid book “Uncertain Manifesto” is haunted by the philosopher’s encounters with fascism, among much else, as these illustrated panels show.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Uncertain Manifesto,” a recently translated book from the Swiss-French writer and artist Frédéric Pajak, is a hybrid, both in form and function. His black-and-white sketches illustrate a collection of stray thoughts — “snippets of adventure, random memories, maxims, ghosts, forgotten heroes, trees, the raging sea,” as he explains in his preface. But one recurring motif is the story of Walter Benjamin, the German Jewish philosopher and cultural critic who witnessed Europe being consumed by fascism. In Pajak’s drawings, Benjamin experiences the maddened crowds, exiles himself to Ibiza and then Paris, and eventually commits suicide during a failed escape from the Continent. In an email, Pajak explained his fascination with the philosopher: “The past has two faces: the past of the victors and the past of the vanquished, erased from history. But, history can only exist if the present repairs the trauma of the past. The injustice done to the victims of history must be repaired, even and especially if, as Benjamin wrote, ‘it is more difficult to honor the memory of the nameless than that of the known.’”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/28/books/review/28BackPage-01/28BackPage-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/04/28/books/review/28BackPage-01/28BackPage-01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 680}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/28/books/review/28BackPage-01/28BackPage-01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 904, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/28/books/review/28BackPage-01/28BackPage-01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1807, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/28/books/review/28BackPage-01/28BackPage-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/04/28/books/review/28BackPage-01/28BackPage-01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/28/books/review/28BackPage-01/28BackPage-01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Agony of Walter Benjamin', 'kicker': 'Sketchbook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pajak, Frederic (1955- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Uncertain Manifesto (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-04-26T09:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a247715c-5fb6-54e8-9909-edda9f79170d', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a247715c-5fb6-54e8-9909-edda9f79170d'}, {'abstract': 'In “Doomstead Days,” Brian Teare tracks climate change on his walks. In “Sight Lines,” Arthur Sze balances firing squads and rowboats. In “Brute,” Emily Skaja escapes a violent relationship. In “Hold Sway,” Sally Ball monitors tragedies averted.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/26/books/review/new-poetry-collections-despair.html', 'snippet': 'In “Doomstead Days,” Brian Teare tracks climate change on his walks. In “Sight Lines,” Arthur Sze balances firing squads and rowboats. In “Brute,” Emily Skaja escapes a violent relationship. In “Hold Sway,” Sally Ball monitors tragedies averted.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DOOMSTEAD DAYS By Brian Teare', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/04/28/books/review/28Shortlist/28Shortlist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/04/28/books/review/28S